<description>
Build the woven rectangle determined by the two marked cells on each crossed bar.

Input structure
A black-background grid contains one long horizontal bar and one long vertical bar.
Each bar has a dominant base color and exactly two same-colored marker pixels.

Rule
The horizontal marker pair gives the output column span.  From the horizontal bar
row, walk toward the farther vertical marker; rows and columns alternate every
other cell.  Even step/even offset cells use the horizontal marker color until
the nearer vertical marker is reached, then use the vertical marker color.
Odd row steps use the horizontal base on marker columns and the original
crossing color on spacer columns.  Spacer columns on even row steps use the
vertical base, except at the switch row where they use the crossing color.

Key insight
The original cross cell records which base color wins at base/base lattice
crossings; the nearer vertical marker is the color-switch boundary.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "one horizontal bar and one vertical bar crossing on the background; each bar has a dominant base color and exactly two same-colored marker pixels",
  "transform": "replace the scene with the woven rectangle whose columns span the horizontal marker pair and whose rows run from the horizontal bar to the farther vertical marker; alternate rows and columns every other cell, switch marker color at the nearer vertical marker, and use the original crossing color at base/base lattice intersections"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10-25 rows x 10-15 cols, background black
  colors: exactly 4 distinct non-black colors (h_base, h_marker, v_base, v_marker)
  bars:
    h_bar: one contiguous horizontal bar spanning most/all of grid width at row h_row
    v_bar: one contiguous vertical bar spanning most/all of grid height at col v_col
    crossing: bars intersect at (h_row, v_col); cross_color is one of {h_base, v_base}
  markers:
    h_marker: exactly 2 cells of h_marker color in h_bar; rest are h_base (+ cross_color at crossing)
    v_marker: exactly 2 cells of v_marker color in v_bar; rest are v_base (+ cross_color at crossing)
    v_markers_same_side: both v_marker cells lie strictly on one side (above or below) of h_row
    h_markers_placement: both h_marker cells may straddle v_col or be on same side
  output:
    rect: rows from h_row to far_v_marker, cols from left_h_marker to right_h_marker
    size: (far_step+1) rows x (h_span+1) cols; far_step 4-15, h_span 2-6
    pattern: |
      even-step rows alternate h_marker/v_base (or cross_color at near_step row);
      odd-step rows are all h_base; marker color switches from h_marker to v_marker at near_step
    near_step: 1-13 (distance from h_row to nearer v_marker)
    v_bar_extends_both_sides: v_bar extends >=2 rows on non-marker side of h_row [observed]
    non_marker_side_pure_base: v_bar cells on non-marker side are only v_base [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick 4 distinct non-black colors for h_base, h_marker, v_base, v_marker; pick cross_color in {h_base, v_base}
    2. Choose grid size (H x W) in [10..25] x [10..15]
    3. Choose output rect dimensions: h_span in [2..6], far_step in [4..15]; near_step in [1..far_step-1]
    4. Place h_row and v_col so the output rect (h_span+1 cols wide, far_step+1 rows tall) fits in grid
    5. Build h_bar along h_row spanning >=W-2 columns including the output column range; place h_marker at left/right of output rect
    6. Build v_bar along v_col spanning >=H-4 rows including h_row; place v_markers at near/far steps from h_row (same side)
    7. Set cross cell to cross_color; fill remaining bar cells with respective base colors
    8. Output is deterministic from input via the solver weaving rule
